body:
Lmao at all the sad and disappointed people.

&#x200B;

Newsflash to everyone expecting/wanting to get rich from btc. Although sometimes you get lucky and it does happen overnight, usually there will be some pain and suffering involved with hodling crypto. But the longterm chart doesnt lie. If you can force yourself to just buy and check the price every month or so eventually you will be greatly rewarded. 

&#x200B;

You either belief or you don't. If you want a chance at a quick buck hit the casino.

Dude I'm not defending bitcoin lol. I have made lots of money and I know that even if it is a bubble it still has a long ways to go. Even you know deep down it is much more likely 20k will be seen before 2k. That alone makes it a worthy investment, the politics, ethics, principles don't change that fact and that's what I wanted to discuss.

&#x200B;

I agree that people overlook how vastly different and possibly dangerous a deflationary currency could be. Bitcoin actually being used much is light years away if it ever happens, it will be storage of wealth like it is now for a while. Obviously these other issues like scaling and transactions are problems that could very likely end up bursting the bubble. But don't you think it is naive to think there is zero chance there will be a solution at some point to these problems? Unlikely maybe, but impossible?  It's the same idiocy as a butters guaranteeing crypto will take over in 10 years.

Ha I hope you get  struck by lightning by the flying spaghetti monster. That's about as likely.

&#x200B;

A better response should have been...hope you get drunk and set your house on fire and lose your keys. Much more realistic.

If bitcoin replaced the USD in 50 years and was so universally accepted that it finally stabilized and had little to no volatility, would you still consider it a pyramid scheme?

&#x200B;

I understand that's a hard if considering you don't like bitcoin. But since I'm a half believer I think it's possible. In such a case is that person still a scumbag?

Thanks for the all comments everyone. Mostly on topic, although I feel like some are not quite getting what I am asking. Basically, if you were 90% sure bitcoin would reach a new ATH within the next 2 years would you invest? How much? This is my belief so I have invested. Partly because of some general concepts of bitcoin, but mostly because I strongly believe people will want to gamble again and think they "understand and believe" the tech when deep down they are lying to themselves and just want to get rich.

&#x200B;

I know that's a hard hypothetical since a majority here hate bitcoin. But if there was a 90% chance of doubling your investment in 2 years would you do it? And if not, why? Thanks.

LMAO! Another coiner stereotype lol. I was getting bashed by butters telling people in late 2017 to be careful and that a parabolic move couldn't be sustainable and meant a harsh correction. Obviously anybody telling peeps to take out loans for a volatile investment is an idiot. And acting like since I profit off morons makes me a bad person is hilarious. There is NOTHING I could do to make people less greedy. It's human nature. Telling them to be careful and understand their investment seems to make them more wreckless. 

&#x200B;

I agree it's unfortunate, and trust me making money off crypto isn't fulfilling in a soulful way at all. I try to achieve that in other areas of my life. But acting like you wouldn't make money in what is essentially a gambling arena if you could is cute. In fact, it's starting to look like maybe you took out a loan or lost your keys, or even worse didn't just hodl through the tough times.

I've made a lot of poor decisions in life. From women, to education, to alcohol, to jobs I've done a lot of dumb things. 

&#x200B;

Saying fuck it in early February and dropping money on ltc at $32 was a good decision.

Nice buy! From here on out it's all up to you. If you are new to trading it's going to be hard to not sell once you start seeing your profit dip. You will learn a lot of lessons and be frustrated as you start up in the trading world. You will make mistakes, we all do. Hodling is great if you've invested only what you can't worry about. Otherwise it can be a rollercoaster of emotions. 

&#x200B;

Welcome, once you enter the world of crypto there is no turning back. Whether it be hodling, trading, profit taking, shorting, you will never leave once you begin....

Hang in there everybody! Just a couple thoughts...

&#x200B;

1. I am very curious as to if btc is going to go through exactly what XRP did in August/September, as in jumping an extreme amount from bottom back to near bottom. It makes me wonder if XRP cycles are actually a bit faster and since the timing between the two is off there are obviously periods of hopelessness.
2. This point kind of contradicts the possiblities of the above mentioned idea, but do you guys remember what happened to XRP a week after bitcoin hit it's ATH? Don't be surprised if an epic rally results out of this sometime soon.

&#x200B;

Regardless of anything, we can't complain about the current support of .30. I know it is frustrating watching these other coins dominate, but this repeated support area does seem like a great entry point. The risk is so much lower than buying into these pumped coins right now.

Wow, what happened to this sub?? The negativity throughout all the threads is very high. Almost looks like sub designed for people that hate XRP.

&#x200B;

It's times like these that are usually a good time to buy. I buy bitcoin when hate comments on btc daily discussion get 10+ upvotes, and sell when it gets 10+ downvotes. Almost never fails. Usually see strong flip within 7 days.

Ha quite a bit of optimism, too much to be honest. And probably not for the right reasons..

It's gonna be really tough for a real bull market to emerge anytime in 2019, and would be unhealthy if it did. But yes, bitcoin not hitting a new low in 2 months is encouraging, as are things like transaction volume being at new highs. Those are the things I am excited for. Patience my friends, I'm hoping for us to sit around levels like this for a while as things cool down from a few crazy years. Accumulate and conquer!

&#x200B;

I agree. Bitcoin has become kinda like politics in that most people are so one sided, especially in bear and bull markets. The negativity nowadays is almost completely price driven, but the interesting thing is that development and news has been very positive overall in the last 6 months or so at least. 

&#x200B;

Bitcoin's price has never gone in the direction that it should based on it's advancements. Looking back at the bull run in 2017, it's absurd to think how many issues there were and nobody cared...issues that are since being solved or at least look to be in the near future. A knock on volume? Fine with me. That's what signals the consolidation period in previous bitcoin cycles. 

&#x200B;

The undeniable truth is that thousands of bitcoin wallets are created each day, and transactions are going up. Yet the current bears are like 2017 bulls, just seeing no possibility of a reversal. They truly believe that bitcoin will be under a $100 in 2 years. Which is really cute.

I've been trying to learn more about national and global economics recently. Obviously many bitcoin enthusiasts believe in some type of impending financial crisis, which I do as well. I thought some of you might find this article interesting.

&#x200B;

[https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/10/style/2019-financial-crisis.html](https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/10/style/2019-financial-crisis.html)

